## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-cloud module design, provider aliasing, and policy-driven governance at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform multi-provider modules
- Provider aliasing and per-tenant scoping
- Open Policy Agent integration with terraform show -json plan
- CI gates and fail-fast

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module interface skeleton (Terraform-like)
module data_lake {
  source = ./modules/data_lake
  tenants = {
    t1 = { cloud = aws, region = us-east-1 }
    t2 = { cloud = gcp, region = us-central1 }
  }
  quotas = {
    t1 = { data_lake_size_gb = 100 }
    t2 = { data_lake_size_gb = 200 }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Sample OPA policy skeleton
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.resource_changes[_].change.actions[_] == create
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-cloud drift?
- How would you version and rollback policy changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of module composition, workspace-based state, and simple CI gates in a beginner-friendly way. It ensures the candidate can wire a wrapper for consistent tagging across environments and validate constraints early in CI.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform modules and wrappers
- Variable validation for environment constraints
- Workspaces and workspace-aware backends
- Per-env state isolation with a single backend
- GitHub Actions gates around terraform plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// modules/env-wrapper/variables.tf
variable "environment" {
  type = string
  description = "Environment name (dev, prod)"
  validation {
    condition = contains(["dev","prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "environment must be 'dev' or 'prod'"
  }
}
variable "cost_center" {
  type = string
}
```

```javascript
// modules/env-wrapper/main.tf
locals {
  common_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  }
}
```
